## Steps, as designed and as run

### STEP 1 — the PKI provisioning path: enrol a user with the local CA and get its credentials off the DUT

The authored precondition's commands were used as the starting point. **Three of them do not
exist in this form on this build**, and the differences are findings in their own right because
anyone following the authored text will hit them.

**(a) `crypto pki` is EXEC-only here, not configuration.** The authored precondition shows
`crypto pki trustpoint local` and `crypto pki enroll local` as configuration lines. On
`sin_x96056-20261019-27`:

```
awplus(config)#crypto pki enroll local user cktls
                          ^
% Invalid input detected at '^' marker.

awplus(config)#crypto pki  ?
  trustpoint  Create or modify a trustpoint        <- config mode offers ONLY this

awplus#crypto pki  ?                                <- exec mode has the rest
  authenticate  Authenticate a root certifcate
  enroll        Enroll the local server or a user to a trustpoint
  export        Export a trustpoint certificate
  import        Import a trustpoint certificate
```

**(b) `crypto pki enroll local user <u>` asks for an export passphrase, twice, as free text.**
Not a (y/n) confirmation, so no existing console driver could answer it: `cfg.py` waits for a
device prompt that never comes, times out after 90 s, and the device prints `% Aborted.`. This
is why `tools/ckcon.py` gained an **`@ask`** directive in this run — send the command raw, then
send each answer after the output next goes quiet. With it:

```
14:35:40 >>> crypto pki enroll local user cktls (then 2 prompted answer(s))
crypto pki enroll local user cktls

Enter an export passphrase, or "abort" to cancel.

Enter the export passphrase again.

Generating a user private key for "cktls"...
Successfully enrolled user "cktls".
The PKCS#12 file is ready to export.
awplus#
14:35:44 >>> show crypto pki certificates local
--------------------
```

**(c) The export syntax is different from the authored text, and better.** Authored:
`crypto pki export local pem url flash:/localca.cer` and
`crypto pki export local pkcs12 stre flash:/stre.p12`. Measured here:

```
awplus#crypto pki export local pem  ?
  URL       Destination URL        <- positional; there is NO `url` keyword
  ecdsa     Export certificate for ECDSA key
  rsa       Export certificate for RSA key
  terminal  Export to terminal     <- not in the authored text at all

awplus#crypto pki export local pkcs12 cktls  ?
  ecdsa  Export certificate with ECDSA key     <- a key type is REQUIRED
  rsa    Export certificate with RSA key
awplus#crypto pki export local pkcs12 cktls rsa  ?
  URL  Destination URL
```

**`pem terminal` is the useful discovery**: the CA certificate comes back on the console as text,
so the bench needs no file transfer at all for it. Used here:

```
awplus#crypto pki export local pem terminal rsa
-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
MIIDZjCCAk6gAwIBAgIJAORZbkQf9giCMA0GCSqGSIb3DQEBCwUAMEMxHTAbBgNV
...
```

and the result parses as a real CA, checked with openssl on the box rather than taken on trust:

```
$ openssl x509 -in localca.cer -noout -subject -issuer -dates
subject=O = "Allied Telesis, Inc.", CN = AlliedWarePlusCAA2WQS700B
issuer=O = "Allied Telesis, Inc.", CN = AlliedWarePlusCAA2WQS700B
notBefore=Oct 15 06:32:24 2026 GMT
notAfter=Oct 14 06:32:24 2031 GMT
```

Self-signed, five-year validity, and **the unit's own serial `A2WQS700B` is inside the CN** — so
this certificate demonstrably came from this DUT and not from anywhere else.

The user's PKCS#12 needs a destination URL, and TFTP straight to the box worked:

```
awplus#crypto pki export local pkcs12 cktls rsa tftp://10.38.22.33/cktls-ec.p12
Copying...
Successful operation
```

⚠️ Recorded because it will confuse the next reader: **the pkcs12 export does NOT re-ask for the
passphrase** (it is already bound into the bundle from enrolment), so the answer supplied for it
was typed at the exec prompt instead and rejected — the three
`user.err IMISH: Command [ckbench2026] failed` lines in the health sample are that, and nothing
else. No device state was touched by them.

**STEP 1 verdict: PASS.** The whole provisioning path works: a user is enrolled against the
pre-existing `local` CA, `show crypto pki certificates local` shows a complete chain (leaf
`/O=AlliedWare Plus/CN=cktls` issued by `/O=Allied Telesis, Inc./CN=AlliedWarePlusCAA2WQS700B`),
the CA exports as parseable PEM and the user identity exports as a PKCS#12 the supplicant can
load.

