## A measurement instrument was corrected mid-case

**`wpasup.py` graded the control leg as `timeout` while an EAP-Failure frame sat in its own
capture.** The verdict was being read from `wpa_supplicant`'s `CTRL-EVENT-*` stream, which it
does not always emit; the pcap had the answer all along. That is an instrument reporting silence
while holding the evidence, and it is the same family of error as grading a run on a `show`
command instead of on the wire.

Fixed: when the event stream yields no verdict, the verdict is now taken **from the frames**, and
the RESULT line says which source it came from —
`got failure (from the WIRE (EAP-Failure frame; no CTRL-EVENT line))`. The control re-ran and
graded MATCH. **The fix does not paper over step 2**: that leg has no EAP-Success and no
EAP-Failure frame either, so it still grades `timeout`, honestly.

Two smaller tool improvements in the same pass, both of which this case needed: `wpasup.py` now
saves the full `wpa_supplicant` output beside the pcap and prints its tail whenever a run did not
do what `--expect` said (without it, step 2 would have been an unexplained timeout), and the
decoder now prints the **EAP length and the EAP-TLS flags** (`0x20 S`, `0xc0 LM msglen=2520`,
`<- EMPTY ACK`), which is what made the stall legible at all.

